<?nvs
// docs/spec/01-core-library.md § 12's decoders, answering octets rather than
// text, and ADR 0102's route capture decoded the same way.
//
// This is a stand-in, for the reason `examples/stream.nvs` states in full:
// `tools/loop.py`'s `begin` walks the goal's `files` list before it builds
// anything, so a fixture that is not on disk costs the whole acceptance run its
// measurement rather than one check.
//
// The rows this is written against are `Core\Uri`'s two decoders. They answer
// `string` today, so a percent-escape that is not valid UTF-8 has no answer
// they can give: `%ff%fe%fd` is three octets and not text, and ADR 0009 § 1
// guarantees a `string` is valid UTF-8 by construction. The decided shape is
// that both rows answer `bytes` and a caller wanting text writes `as string`,
// which is ADR 0009 § 3's checked conversion and throws exactly where the
// member throws now — so no program is denied an answer it could have used.
// `encodeComponent` and `encodeFormValue` are untouched: they take text and
// answer text, and they are this class's two `Qual::Launder` rows.
//
// What replaces this, when the rows change type, is the program the check's
// three frozen lines describe:
//
//   1. `%ff%fe%fd` decodes to the three octets it names, printed as hex, which
//      is an answer only a `bytes` return can carry.
//   2. A query string's name and its value go through the same decoder, so a
//      form field carrying octets survives being read as one.
//   3. A route capture spelled `%20` reaches the handler as a space — the
//      capture is decoded once, at the door, and the handler sees text.

echo "examples/octets.nvs: Core\\Uri's decoders still answer string", "\n";
